SUMMARY:Linguistic Internalism and Externalism: Bridging Medieval and Contemporary Thought
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a one-day workshop on linguistic internalism and externalism featuring specialists in medieval and analytic philosophy. \nProgram: \nWelcome and Introduction to the Topic (9.00 am – 9.30 am) \nSimone Luigi Migliaro (Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin – University of Toronto) \nPaper I (9.30 am – 10.50 am) \nGiorgio Pini (Fordham University) \nEssential Order\, Intuition\, and Abstraction: Duns Scotus and the Internalism/Externalism Divide \nDiscussant: Zain Raza (University of Toronto) \nCoffee Break (10.50 am – 11.10 am) \nPaper II (11.10 am – 12.30 pm)  \nGyula Klima (Fordham University) \nOckham’s Externalism vs Aquinas’ Hyper-Externalism \nDiscussant: Nathan Charlow (University of Toronto) \nLunch (12.30 pm – 2.00 pm) \nPaper III (2.00 pm – 3.20 pm) \nClaude Panaccio (Université du Québec à Montréal) \nA Medieval Debate on Semantic Externalism: Adam Wodeham vs William of Ockham \nDiscussant: Simona Vucu (University of Toronto) \nPaper IV (3.20 pm – 4.40 pm) \nCalvin Normore (University of California\, Los Angeles) \nMental Language: The “King’s” Road  \nDiscussant: Benj Hellie (University of Toronto) \nCoffee Break (4.40 pm – 5.00 pm) \nPaper V (5.00 pm – 6.20 pm) \nSimone Luigi Migliaro (Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin – University of Toronto) \nSingular Terms without Singular Concepts: Two Externalist Interpretations of Nicholas of Amsterdam \nDiscussant: Diana Raffman (University of Toronto) \nConcluding Remarks (6.20 pm – 6.50 pm) \nPeter King (University of Toronto) \nThe workshop is sponsored by the Department of Philosophy\, the Collaborative Specialization in Ancient and Medieval Philosophy\, the Centre for Medieval Studies\, and the Pontifical Institute of Mediaeval Studies. \nView the PDF of the workshop details\nOnline attendance will also be possible for this event. Please contact simoneluigi.migliaro@utoronto.ca for details.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/linguistic-internalism-and-externalism-bridging-medieval-and-contemporary-thought/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building 100
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/adam-naming-the-animals-cropped.png
END:VEVENT
