SUMMARY:History of Philosophy Research Group Talk (Bridger Ehli\, IU-Bloomington)
DESCRIPTION:The History of Modern Philosophy Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Bridger Ehli\, an assistant professor in the Department of Philosophy at the University of Indiana – Bloomington. His research concerns themes in epistemology\, metaphysics\, and the philosophy of mind in early modern European philosophy. He is especially interested in early modern discussions of skepticism\, especially as they relate to questions about mental representation\, concepts\, and the psychology of belief. Most of his work has focused on Hume’s contributions to these issues. In a forthcoming book\, he argues that much of Hume’s skepticism arises from his thinking about the limits of intelligibility rather than those of justified belief. \nDetails to follow.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/history-of-philosophy-research-group-talk-bridger-ehli-iu-bloomington/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/ehli_bridger_225-x-225.png
END:VEVENT
